ACCESS AND ETIQUETTE

A - A few words about access and etiquette

:A
A FEW WORDS ABOUT ACCESS AND ETIQUETTE

An RCP/M is a shared computer, and the sysop has to strike a balance between
providing useful facilities and keeping the machine available for the next
caller.

Ordinarily you can read the public collections, download their files and
execute approved programs. You cannot run every COM file you see in the
directory. The sysop chooses which programs are suitable for online use; the
supplied AUTH facility requires the executable's DU to be readable but not
caller-writable and the file to have the appropriate approval attribute.

WORK is the exception to the normal read-only library arrangement. Programs
use it for temporary data. It is not a personal disk or a place to keep
software between calls. There is no general caller copy command or direct
upload into WORK on the standard system.

If a program is unavailable for online execution, download it for your own
machine rather than trying to circumvent the board's restrictions. Ask the
sysop if you think it would make a useful addition to the online collection.

If you have the only caller connection, bear in mind that someone else may be
waiting. Don't leave it occupied unnecessarily. Be considerate in the forums,
keep private matters out of public messages, and give sufficient details when
reporting a problem: the command you used, the volume mounted, the terminal
type and what actually happened.
